<<<
as the cognate, or the internal, or the adverbial, object or accusative: is playing whist (cognate); I hate whist (direct); lived a good life (internalor cognate); spent his life well (direct); looked daggers (adverbialor cognate).
===
as the *cognate*, or the *internal*, or the *adverbial*, *object* or *accusative*: is playing *whist* (cognate); I hate *whist* (direct); lived a good *life* (internal or cognate); spent his *life* well (direct); looked *daggers* (adverbial or cognate).
>>>

<<<
In the last example daggers is a metaphor for a look of a certain kind, & therefore cognate with the verb.
===
In the last example *daggers* is a metaphor for a look of a certain kind, & therefore cognate with the verb.
>>>

